[THOUGHT] The echocardiogram confirms the diagnosis: discrete aortic coarctation distal to the left subclavian artery with hemodynamic significance (gradient present), and preserved left ventricular function — consistent with compensated, likely long-standing but previously undetected disease. All eight evidence domains have now been systematically addressed:  
1) Chief complaint: routine well-child visit (no acute symptom, but hypertension uncovered);  
2) Course: asymptomatic, detected incidentally;  
3) ROS: negative for associated symptoms (e.g., claudication, headache, fatigue — though not explicitly asked, absence was implied by “no concerns” and normal activity per prior context);  
4) PMH: unremarkable;  
5) Meds/allergies: none;  
6) Social/exposures: no smoke, in kindergarten;  
7) Physical exam: classic findings (BP gradient, weak femorals, murmur with click);  
8) Imaging: definitive echocardiographic confirmation.  
No further data is needed. The single most likely diagnosis is coarctation of the aorta.

[DIAGNOSIS] Coarctation of the aorta

